{
 "id": "2024-gu-front",
 "name": "Front Garden Visual Assessment (rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the public visual impact assessment in Gu, Han, Zhao, Zhang, Yang & Zhang (2024), \"Is there any difference between the owners and the public in their visual impact assessments? A case study of the front garden of multi-storey residential buildings\", PLOS ONE 19(1): e0296519. The study used nine rendered scenes (A–I) of the private front gardens of ground-floor flats in Chinese multi-storey housing. The authors modelled them in SketchUp 2021 and rendered them in Lumion 11, after nine typical gardens that owners had shared. The scenes vary garden size, plant type, vertical structure, number of plant colours, plant coverage and fence material. Participants give gender, age group, education and income, then score each picture from 0 (strongly dislike) to 5 (strongly like). In the paper, 256 participants in Jiangsu took part. Garden F scored highest (3.95) and garden A lowest (2.37). The pictures are the authors' Fig 2 (CC BY 4.0), used as published with credit to the authors. This template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the pictures are shown on screen instead of in an A4 photo book, and anyone with the link can take part; (2) the question \"Do you own a ground-floor flat with a front garden?\" is our addition, to split owners from the public as the paper does; (3) the question wording and the income threshold label (RMB 47,498 per year) are ours; (4) pictures appear two per page, like the A4 sheets, in a random order for each participant; (5) the letter labels were removed from our copies of the figure.",
